WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.300
How Art Can Transform the Internet
---- Subtitled by @londreterreando ----

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:07.900
In Thomas Pynchon's sprawling epic
post-modern novel Gravity's Rainbow,

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:13.100
there's a sequence in which the main
character is interrogated by the US government

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.400
under the influence of sodium amytal,
commonly called "truth serum".

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.300
The writing slips into 
several dream-like rips on language.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.000
In one telegram from Wisconsin,
launches the main character

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.100
into an obsessive meditation

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.000
on the possible constructions
of a six-word sequence:

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.700
"You never did. The Kenosha kid".

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:41.500
As more and more examples are presented, 
each with different punctuations,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.700
we become aware of
a key post-modern truth

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:48.500
that words can point toward reality,

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.000
that they can never get there;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.700
they always mean different things
in different contexts.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.600
But just how many different
 contexts are there?

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.900
Well, Pynchon only gives us seven.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.900
There are far too many
punctuation marks and emphases,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.700
far too many combinations
for a single writer to catalog them all.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.200
Besides, in the aesthetic universe
of the novel,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.700
cataloging them all would be
exhaustive and redundant.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.000
Enter the Internet and
a man named Darius Kazemi.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.000
"Hi, everybody.”

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.300
He makes what he likes to call
"weird internet stuff".

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.000
"I make what I like to call 
'weird internet stuff',

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.600
you have watched it recently
come to accept is internet art."

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.700
Last year Darius made a Twitter bot

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.300
that did what the writer 
Thomas Pynchon can't ever do.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.100
It automatically generates
a different construction

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.100
of the "You never did. The Kenosha Kid"
sequence every two hours.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.700
As of this video, the account
has already tweeted 8600 times,

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.500
and with the number
of permutations possible

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.800
it could continue for years
without exhausting everyone.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.100
Go there sometime.
Try reading a few out loud.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.900
The effect of Pynchon's
language experiment

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.300
is compounded, updated.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:05.100
The truth it wants to communicate
is received in a new way,

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.700
and the exhaustiveness that would have been
inappropriate in a book

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.400
is made novel and strangely vital
in this new form.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.000
I think what this shows us is
that the aesthetics of Internet art,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.400
art made with and for
and on the Internet,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.600
won't conform to any that we've
known before.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.600
And that's fitting because
a lot of internet art is made

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.700
unlike any art that came before.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:34.700
Because Amy's twitter bots are made
with paintbrushes or musical instruments.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.500
They're made it 
with algorithms with code.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.500
This is what the Kenosha Kid
Twitter bot really looks like.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.400
Is there any beauty in that?

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.800
What about this bot? 
No, it's just a set of instructions

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.600
Here's what those instructions do.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.800
This program and these rules
perform a simple task:

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.300
they access the Texas
Department of Criminal Justice

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:10.000
and pour from the public last word
statements of death row inmates

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:14.100
any sentence that contains
the word "love".

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.800
This art pieces are out to make
an overtly political statement

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.700
on capital punishment
like all good art.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:23.000
This project organizes elements
in the world around it

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.700
helping audience see
and feel in a new way.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.600
The difference is that
the world Kazemi's commented on

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.200
isn't the physical world, not really.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.800
It's the digital world,
the world of the Internet,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:40.100
and indeed much of our digital
Internet art is focused on itself

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.100
on the visible and, 
more importantly,

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.800
invisible aspects of the network
that's all around us.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.900
"Computation is now everywhere;
it is layered over everything

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.600
and we are living inside
that computer as well."

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.900
That's James Bridle,
a critic in digital artist.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.800
Bridle did a project
recently called Rainbow Planes,

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.700
a project that started when he noticed
some odd artifacts from Google Earth

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.000
of planes with rainbow-after images.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.300
"It's a glitch, but like all
true glitches,

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.900
it's not just a mistake,

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.500
it's a kind of an opportunity
to look through

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.600
into the underlying systems
that produce this image."

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.100
In this case it shows us that
the satellites composing these images

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.700
aren't using cameras but rather
are collecting data from a broad swath

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.200
of the electromagnetic spectrum.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.300
"And they record red,
green and blue separately

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.400
when they make these data images.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.400
And so occasionally you find
within satellite images these artifacts

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.200
which produced by fast moving objects."

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.100
Bridals's digital art wants to help us see
the way technology sees us:

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.000
how hugely powerful systems
have moved slowly

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.600
into our lives without much protest.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.100
These systems are not like us:
we see with eyes, they see with data;

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.500
we think with minds,
they think with algorithms.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:01.300
Art like this shows us that our increasing
dependence on sophisticated technologies

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:07.800
comes at the cost of substituting
our values and our motives for theirs.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.000
So maybe the aesthetic value
of Internet art

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.600
is measured by its ability
to help us see the Internet,

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:20.400
just as a novel can help us see language
or an image can help us see reality.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.800
That's just an idea and I can't say
how valid it is for you,

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:30.500
but I think we do have to try to develop
an aesthetic for this new digital art,

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.500
away by which we can determine
which of it is good, which of it is crap,

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.100
and which we consider truly essential.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:39.600
Because what we think is great art

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:43.700
speaks ultimately to what
we think is valuable in culture.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:48.100
And articulating our values about
the Internet, the global network,

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:51.800
in the years before
it consumes everything

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.300
might just be the most important thing

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:58.400
our generation could ever do.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.000
Hey, everybody! Thanks for watching.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.900
I'm getting the scene
just before sunset here.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.600
